,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841697358,"data":" How do I get crispy skin on a salmon fillet? \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841713099,"data":"---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841717284,"data":" Sure, here's how you can achieve that:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841720718,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841723988,"data":"1. Season the Salmon: Start by seasoning your salmon with salt and pepper to give it a nice flavor. You can also add some lemon juice or herbs like dill or parsley for extra taste.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841727638,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841730729,"data":"2. Preheat the Pan: Place a large skillet over medium heat and add a tablespoon of oil. Once the pan is hot, carefully place the salmon fillets in it. Make sure not to overcrowd the pan as this can cause uneven cooking.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841734455,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.84173763,"data":"3. Cook the Salmon: Sear the salmon for about 4-5 minutes per side, depending on its thickness. This will create a nice crust on the outside while keeping the inside moist.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841741262,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841744403,"data":"4. Flip and Cook Another 3-4 Minutes: After searing, flip the salmon and cook for a\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841747746,"data":"================================================================================ \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841751245,"data":" Should I add oil to the water when boiling pasta? \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841754583,"data":"---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841757857,"data":" No, you should not add oil to the water when boiling pasta. The water needs to be cold and fresh for optimal cooking results. Adding oil can cause the pasta to become greasy and may lead to uneven cooking or even make it difficult to remove from the pot.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841765506,"data":"================================================================================ \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841768043,"data":" How do I write a unit test for a small Python function? \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841770678,"data":"---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841785103,"data":" To write a unit test for a small Python function, you can use the built-in `unittest` module. Here's an example:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841789368,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841792868,"data":"```python\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841796528,"data":"import unittest\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841800298,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841803804,"data":"def add(a, b):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841807114,"data":"    return a + b\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841810747,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841814305,"data":"class TestAddFunction(unittest.TestCase):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841817616,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841820697,"data":"    def test_add(self):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841823884,"data":"        self.assertEqual(add(2, 3), 5)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841827703,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841831444,"data":"    def test_add_with_zero(self):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841835054,"data":"        self.assertEqual(add(0, 0), 0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841837977,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841840941,"data":"    def test_negative_numbers(self):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841844063,"data":"        with self.assertRaises(ValueError):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841851683,"data":"            add(-1, -2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841854966,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841858166,"data":"if __name__ == '__main__':\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841861249,"data":"    unittest.main()\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841864401,"data":"```\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841867333,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841872517,"data":"In this example, we define a class `TestAddFunction` that inherits from `unittest.TestCase`. The `test_add` method is a test case for the `add` function. We use the `assertEqual` method t\n"}
