,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841881193,"data":" Explain what compound interest is in simple terms. \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841885193,"data":"---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841888312,"data":" Compound interest is like earning money that grows over time because you earn interest on both the principal amount (the initial amount) and any accrued interest. It's really cool because it can help your money grow much faster than if you just put it into an account without adding interest. For example, let's say you have $100 and you deposit it into a savings account with 5% annual interest rate. After one year, you'll have $105 ($100 + $5). But if you add another year of interest to this, you'd get $110.25 ($105 + $5.75), which is more than the original $100. This means your money has grown significantly faster than if you had simply kept it there.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":598.841893151,"data":"================================================================================ \n"}
